<p>In the latest turn, Iran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi traveled from Pakistan to Russia on April 27 after shuttle diplomacy that also took him through Oman, while planned in-person talks with U.S. envoys in Pakistan were put on hold. President Trump said Tehran could contact Washington directly by phone if it wanted to negotiate an end to the war, after canceling a trip by U.S. envoys to Islamabad and citing confusion inside Iran’s leadership. Pakistan has kept up its mediation effort and remains a key channel for messages between Tehran and Washington, with Araghchi meeting senior Pakistani civilian and military leaders during his brief return to Islamabad. Uncertainty remains high, but the talks now seem to be shifting toward indirect exchanges, including messages relayed through mediators and Araghchi’s talks with Russian President Vladimir Putin.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qec-LglSGMNdFLh50aHkAqzyuqT4hQ-5dChTYRx0JVrfQWcOXb6qETgoTUBo3V2ajH0iMZ-JrYvB97HupVKKPAy01PmxFJA0s0xGx3JQBGLb' alt='Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi is greeted by officials during his visit to Russia for talks with President Vladimir Putin in St. Petersburg on April 27, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan channel: President Trump said Islamabad would remain part of the process even if talks continue by telephone instead of immediate face-to-face meetings.</li><li>Iran messages: Iran reportedly sent written messages to the United States through Pakistan outlining red lines, including on nuclear issues and the Strait of Hormuz, though Fars said these were not formal negotiations.</li><li>Hormuz proposal: Axios, as cited by multiple outlets, reported that Iran sent a proposal to reopen the Strait of Hormuz and end the war while postponing nuclear negotiations until a later stage.</li><li>Moscow meeting: Araghchi said his meeting with President Vladimir Putin would be a good opportunity to discuss developments in the war, underscoring Russia’s role as a diplomatic backstop for Tehran.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: President Trump said the United States canceled its envoys’ Islamabad trip and wants any renewed diplomacy to begin with direct outreach from Tehran, while insisting Iran cannot have a nuclear weapon. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/trump-tells-fox-news-iran-can-call-us-if-it-wants-negotiate'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iran: Iran’s diplomatic push centered on consultations in Pakistan, Oman, and Russia, with Araghchi seeking support from senior officials and discussing both the war and maritime security issues. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/iran-israel-war-live-updates-april-26-2026-usa-donald-trump-west-asia-crisis-conflict/article70907623.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Pakistani leaders have kept trying to broker a second round of U.S.-Iran contacts and have exchanged proposals and messages with both sides as part of their mediation effort. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/iran-foreign-minister-abbas-araghchi-pakistan-donald-trump-peace-talks/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Russia: Moscow prepared to host Araghchi for talks with President Putin, signaling continued Russian engagement as Tehran looks for outside backing while direct U.S. talks remain paused. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/iran-us-war-russia-putin-to-meet-abbas-araghchi-11413274#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/irans-top-diplomat-returns-pakistan-islamabad-races-save-132393186'>Iran&#x27;s top diplomat briefly returns to Pakistan but Trump says the sides can talk by phone</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/4/27/iran-war-live-araghchi-to-meet-putin-trump-says-tehran-can-call-for-talks'>Iran war live: Araghchi to meet Putin; Trump says Tehran can call for talks</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/ar